First, convert the distances from miles to feet (1 mile = 5280 ft).

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Steve's distance} &= 3\text{ mi}\times 5280\frac{\text{ft}}{\text{mi}} = 15\,840\text{ ft},\\[4pt]
\text{Tim's distance}   &= 2\text{ mi}\times 5280\frac{\text{ft}}{\text{mi}} = 10\,560\text{ ft}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Next, compute each travel time using \( \text{time} = \dfrac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}} \).

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Steve's time} &= \frac{15\,840\text{ ft}}{440\ \frac{\text{ft}}{\text{min}}}
                     = \frac{15\,840}{440}\text{ min}
                     = 36\text{ min},\\[4pt]
\text{Tim's time}   &= \frac{10\,560\text{ ft}}{264\ \frac{\text{ft}}{\text{min}}}
                     = \frac{10\,560}{264}\text{ min}
                     = 40\text{ min}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Steve finishes first, so he is the winner.  
The waiting time for the winner is the difference between the two times:

\[
\text{Waiting time}= 40\text{ min} - 36\text{ min}= 4\text{ minutes}.
\]

\[
\boxed{4\text{ minutes}}
\]

Lee’s current time is 38 s.  
When Gerald’s diet had not yet changed, Lee was **2 seconds faster**, so Gerald’s original time was  

\[
38\text{ s}+2\text{ s}=40\text{ s}.
\]

A 10 % increase in speed means his speed becomes \(1.10\) times the original speed.  
Since speed \(v = \dfrac{\text{distance}}{\text{time}}\),

\[
\text{new time}= \frac{\text{distance}}{1.10\,v_{\text{old}}}
               = \frac{1}{1.10}\times\text{old time}
               = \frac{40\text{ s}}{1.10}
               = \frac{40}{1.1}\text{ s}
               = \frac{400}{11}\text{ s}
               \approx 36.36\text{ s}.
\]

\[
\boxed{\,\approx 36.36\text{ seconds}\,}
\]

